# neutrality.tokens.example
#
# Template for the banned-token list read by scripts/neutrality-check.mjs.
# This `.example` file is tracked because it names nothing. Your real list is
# never tracked, in this repository or any other.
#
# Where it goes
# -------------
# OUTSIDE every repository. `sh scripts/setup.sh` creates it for you at
#
#   ~/.config/engineering-governance/tokens
#
# and `--tokens <path>` or $GOVERNANCE_TOKENS override that. Do not copy it
# into a working tree, even an ignored one: a file of real identifiers there is
# one `git add -f` or one lost ignore rule from a permanent public commit, and
# removing a hazard beats defending it with a rule. It does not belong in an
# instance repository either, which may be public.
#
# Not in CI, and not in a repository secret
# ----------------------------------------
# The gate is a capability this engine provides. Running it against a
# particular organisation's identifiers is that organisation's job, run from
# their side, on machines that already hold the names. A CI job needing this
# list would make the engine unbuildable until an adopter existed.
#
# You can only leak identifiers you hold, so someone with no list has nothing
# to leak and is not blocked: the hooks warn and continue, while invoking the
# gate directly still fails closed. A missing or empty list is exit 2, an
# error rather than a pass.
#
# Format
# ------
# One token per line. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored.
#
# You only need to list a name ONCE, in any spelling. Matching ignores case,
# punctuation, spacing and camelCase boundaries, so the single token
# `examplecorp` already catches all of these:
#
#   ExampleCorp          Example Corp         example-corp.com
#   EXAMPLECORP_API_KEY  exampleCorpClient    jane@examplecorp.co.uk
#
# Do not list variants. `examplecorp`, `example corp` and `Example-Corp` are
# the same token to the scanner, and duplicates only make the list harder to
# keep true.
#
# Keep tokens distinctive. A token that is also an ordinary English word will
# fire on innocent prose and train you to bypass the gate, which is worse than
# not having it. Prefer `examplecorp` over `example`, and never list a bare
# number like a day rate, which would match any occurrence of that figure.
#
# Multi-word tokens match only when the words are adjacent, so `acme
# industries` does not fire on "industries vary between acme suppliers".

# --- Trading names ------------------------------------------------------------
examplecorp
example corp ltd

# --- Domains you own (the registrable part is enough) -------------------------
# `examplecorp` above already covers examplecorp.com and any address at it.
# Add entries only for domains that do not contain the trading name.
exampleapp
getexample

# --- People: staff and contractor names ---------------------------------------
jane doe

# --- Company registration, VAT and other statutory identifiers ----------------
12345678
gb123456789

# --- Clients and engagements --------------------------------------------------
# The names that must never surface in a shared engine repo.
northwind
acme industries

# --- Products, projects and internal codenames --------------------------------
# Including retired ones. A dead codename still identifies the business.
bluebird
tidewater

# --- Infrastructure names that identify the estate ----------------------------
# Host and shard labels, if yours are meaningful rather than opaque.
onyxhost
coralhost
